Thousands of papers have been published during the last 25 years reporting on the
use and obtention of Kc values and Kc curves for essentially all cultivated crops. However, the objectives and conditions of the various studies have been quite diverse
resulting in sometimes contradictory and competing values for Kc. This presents a clear
disadvantage when assessing the transferability of Kc values to different environments
as detailed in Box 5.1. BOX 5.1
Limitations to the transferability of Kc data
Limitations to the transferability of Kc data can be due to a variety of factors,
including:
1. Using an ETo estimation method different than the standard FAO-PM ETo equation;
2. Using non-standard cultivation conditions, such as mulch, plastic tunnels,
intercropping and greenhouses/screen houses;
3. Adopting crop management practices that may cause various types of stresses, e.g.,
relative to the use of fertilizers, chemicals, or wastewater;
4. Insufficient description of the experiments, namely of the plant density, height, and
vigor, and the instrumentation used and its accuracy;
5. Insufficient calibration and validation of models when used for ET estimation. 6. Cropping under soil and water salinity/sodicity stresses;
7. Using non-optimal plant density and/or planting dates;
8. Adopting deficit irrigation;
9. Insufficient size of experimental plots;
10. Inadequate lysimeter management;
11. Using energy balance instrumentation without adopting an appropriate energy
balance closure;
12. Lack of appropriate estimation of soil water fluxes that affect the accuracy of soil
water balance (SWB), particularly the amount of water passing through the bottom
boundary of the control volume, deep percolation and capillary rise;
13. Lack of consideration of possible occurrence of local advection;
14. Lack of correction of weather data collected over non-transpiring or lowtranspiring surfaces; and
15. Using non-calibrated vegetation indices when remote sensing is adopted.
